b='content/os/03-synchronisation/';p=b+'sync-locks.md';q=b+'sync-locks.questions.json';s=['https://docs.kernel.org/locking/locktypes.html'];f=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/futex.2.html'];c=['https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n1570.pdf'];g=['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bminor/glibc/master/nptl/pthread_mutex_unlock.c'];d=['https://www.akkadia.org/drepper/futex.pdf']
edit(p,'On a single-CPU machine, a thread only loses the CPU when an interrupt (such as the timer) fires. Disable interrupts in `lock()`, re-enable them in `unlock()`, and nobody can interleave.','On a single CPU, disabling maskable interrupts can protect a short kernel section against relevant interrupt handlers, provided it cannot block, yield or otherwise schedule and the kernel\'s preemption rules are respected. It does not exclude every context, such as non-maskable interrupts.','Blocking/cooperativescheduling/NMIs invalidateonlyinterruptclaim.',s)
edit(p,'Interrupts lost while disabled (for example from a disk) can be missed or delayed.','Interrupt handling is delayed. Many interrupt sources remain pending, but excessive latency or finite device buffering can still lose events or data.','Maskingdoesnotgenerallydropallinterrupts.',s)
edit(p,'Threads enter in strict FIFO order, so bounded waiting is guaranteed. Linux used ticket spinlocks from 2.6.25 and later moved to queued spinlocks, which are also fair but spin on a per-CPU variable instead of one shared one.','Tickets order admission by ticket allocation, assuming holders and waiters keep progressing and counter wraparound does not alias outstanding tickets. Linux queued spinlocks use queue-based waiting, but their implementation and fairness properties should not be equated with this simple ticket model.','Boundrequiresprogress/counterspace;queuedlocknotuniversallystrictFIFO.',s)
edit(p,'That is exactly the situation inside an OS kernel, where taking a spinlock also disables preemption on that CPU (and interrupts, with `spin_lock_irqsave`, if an interrupt handler shares the lock).','On non-PREEMPT_RT Linux, spinlock_t disables preemption while held; irqsave variants also mask local interrupts. Under PREEMPT_RT, spinlock_t instead has sleeping-lock semantics, while raw_spinlock_t remains a strict spinning lock.','CriticalPREEMPT_RTexception.',s)
edit(p,'But a system call costs far more than an atomic instruction (hundreds of nanoseconds or more versus a few nanoseconds), and most lock operations are uncontended.','Avoiding a kernel transition can reduce uncontended-path overhead; actual costs and contention rates are workload- and platform-dependent.','Unsupporteduniversalbenchmarkcosts.',f)
edit(p,'(`futex_wait` and `futex_wake` stand for thin wrappers around the `futex` syscall.)','(`futex_wait` and `futex_wake` are illustrative wrappers. A real Linux implementation needs a correctly aligned 32-bit futex word compatible with its atomic representation, the appropriate private/shared operation and handling for interruptions, spurious wakes and errors.)','Atomicintsize/alignmentanderrorsnotportableLinuxcontract.',f)
edit(p,'The kernel is involved only when threads actually collide. glibc\'s `pthread_mutex_t` is built this way, and so are most lock implementations on Linux.','This teaching algorithm illustrates the fast-path principle. Ordinary glibc mutexes also use user-space atomic state plus futex slow paths, but exact instructions and paths differ with mutex type, glibc version and optimizations.','Glibcactualunlockexchange notdecrement andspecialtypes.',g)
edit(p,'A lock is correct the moment it exists. Whether it is *fast* depends on **contention**: how often threads want it at the same time.','A correctly implemented lock still needs a correct application protocol: all relevant accesses must participate, lifetimes must be valid and lock ordering must avoid deadlock. Performance then depends partly on contention.','Existenceoflockdoesnotproveprotocol.',c)
edit(p,'When a lock is uncontended, its cost is a few nanoseconds.','No reproducible uncontended-lock timing is supplied here.', 'Removeunsupportedbenchmark.',g)
edit(p,'Per-thread counters summed at the end (sharding) beat any lock.','Per-thread counters combined at the end can reduce contention when delayed aggregation preserves the required semantics.', 'Performanceuniversalandsemanticsunsupported.',c)
edit(p,'Two unrelated variables on the same 64-byte cache line contend even under separate locks.','Independent writes sharing a cache line can cause coherence traffic even under separate locks. Cache-line size is architecture-dependent; 64 bytes is one common example.', 'Read-onlysharingnotcontention andlinesizevaries.',c)
edit(p,'## Key takeaways','> [!note] Content gap\n> No reproducible lock or syscall benchmark accompanies this lesson, so exact nanosecond costs and universal speed-up claims are omitted. The futex code is a teaching sketch, not a portable production mutex.\n\n## Key takeaways','Explicitunverifiedperformancegap.',f)
qe(q,'sync-locks-q3','prompt','An ordinary non-robust, non-PI glibc pthread mutex takes its uncontended user-space fast path for both lock and unlock. How many futex system calls are required?','Specifyordinarytypeandfastpathnotallpthreadtypes.',g)
qe(q,'sync-locks-q3','text','Zero: the uncontended fast path stays in user space','Exactsingleatomicinstructionnotuniversal.',g,option=2)
qe(q,'sync-locks-q3','explanation','Correct. No futex wait or wake is needed on this path. The exact atomic operations depend on implementation and optimization; this does not promise exactly one machine instruction per API call.','Glibcunlockusesexchange andsinglethreadoptimization.',g,option=2)
qe(q,'sync-locks-q3','workedExample','For the stipulated ordinary uncontended fast path, user-space state is sufficient to acquire and release, so no futex syscall is needed. The lesson\'s decrement algorithm illustrates one design; glibc versions and mutex types can use different instructions and additional paths.','Removeincorrectglibcassembly equivalence.',g)
qe(q,'sync-locks-q4','text','After failure sets expected to 1, a later CAS can succeed writing 1 over 1 while A still holds the lock','WeakCAScanfailspuriously;Aschedcanreleasebefore next.',c,option=1)
qe(q,'sync-locks-q4','explanation','Correct as a possible failure: with expected changed to 1, a subsequent successful comparison can enter while the lock is still held. Weak CAS may also fail spuriously; the broken protocol no longer enforces exclusion.','Possibletracevsinevitable nextCAS.',c,option=1)
qe(q,'sync-locks-q8','explanation','Correct when the section is short and the holder can continue on another CPU. The cost trade-off needs measurement; no fixed nanosecond duration is implied.','Unsupportedduration.',s,option=0)
qe(q,'sync-locks-q8','text','A non-threaded hard interrupt handler sharing a short section with other kernel contexts, using the appropriate raw spinlock and interrupt masking','PREEMPTRTthreadedinterrupt/sleeping spinlockexception.',s,option=2)
qe(q,'sync-locks-q11','text','Move a database write outside the critical section if versioning or another protocol preserves ordering and consistency','MovingIOcanbreakcorrectnesswithoutprotocol.',c,option=2)
qe(q,'sync-locks-q11','explanation','Correct under that condition: removing a slow operation can shorten lock hold time. Simply moving it without preserving state ordering may introduce stale or reordered writes.','Cannotrecommendunsafeunlockbeforepersistunqualified.',c,option=2)
qe(q,'sync-locks-q11','workedExample','Sharding can distribute contention if keys are sufficiently spread. Moving I/O out reduces hold time only when the application preserves its ordering and consistency requirements. Padding may reduce false sharing but must be measured. Re-entrancy does not itself improve inter-thread concurrency; widening the locked region can serialize more work.','Removeguaranteedshardfactorandperformance.',c)
